      title: 'Spot Crude Oil Price: West Texas Intermediate (WTISPLC), monthly'
      authors: Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis (FRED)
      year: 2026
      finding: WTI averaged $30.81 a barrel in November 1985 and $11.58 in July 1986, a fall of 62%.
      url: https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/WTISPLC
    - id: carnegie2015
      title: Saudi Arabia and the Shifting Geoeconomics of Oil
      authors: Carnegie Endowment for International Peace
      year: 2015
      finding: OPEC set individual quotas from 1982; members had incentives to disregard them and free-rode while Saudi output contracted from over 10 mb/d in 1980 to 2.4 mb/d in August 1985; when Saudi Arabia raised output, prices collapsed about 50% from 1985 to 1986.
      url: https://carnegieendowment.org/middle-east/research/2015/06/saudi-arabia-and-the-shifting-geoeconomics-of-oil

    text quota "OPEC quotas" #2f6b8f cites ["carnegie2015"] x 820 y 630 font_size 34 hidden true
    text over "members pump over quota" #b5482e x 820 y 592 font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch saudi cites ["carnegie2015"] work ["Saudi output","1980: 10m+ a day","Aug 1985: 2.4m"] x 815 y 480 font_size 26 hidden true
    text drop "−62%" #b5482e cites ["fred_wti"] x 720 y 440 font_size 56 hidden true
    text cue "Each pumps more" #3d3833 x 830 y 570 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Why do rivals fail to cooperate?"

Why do rivals who would all gain from working together so often fail to? Look at the price of oil in the mid-1980s.
    draw axes line_2

B: Since 1982 OPEC, t (@draw quota@)he oil exporters' club, had set quotas: each member promised to pump only  (@highlight line_2@)so much, to hold the price up.

Holding back pays only if everyone holds back. Many  (@draw over@)members kept pumping more than their quota.

B: Saudi A (@draw saudi_box saudi_line_1@)rabia covered for them. It cut its own output from over te (@draw saudi_line_2@)n million barrels a day in 1980 to about two and a (@draw saudi_line_3@) half million by August 1985.

Then it stopped covering, and opened the taps. Your turn: draw what you think the price did next.
    erase saudi quota over

    erase line_2

    ask "Draw the oil price after November 1985." on line_1 from 1985.833 store g
        right: Close: you saw the cliff coming.
        wrong: Here is the real line, beside yours.

From thirty-one dollars a barrel in November 1985 to under twelve by J (@draw drop@)uly 1986. More than half gone, in eight months.

B: Not all of that was OPEC. New oil from the North Sea and Alaska had been pushing prices down for years.
    highlight line_1

But the cartel's own deal fell apart, and every member lost. Yet each one, on its own, ha (@draw cue@)d a good reason to pump more.
    erase drop

B: The easy answer is greed, or stupidity. It takes neither: only a particular shape of game.
    highlight cue effect underline

B: Let's put the deal in a table, find each side's best move, then check how real people play.
    point at.data [1986.2,14] gesture circle

    erase cue

    pause 0.3

## The table
use: payoff_matrix
with:
    row_player: Member A
    col_player: Member B
    row_strategies:
      - Keep quota
      - Pump more
    col_strategies:
      - Keep quota
      - Pump more
    payoffs:
      - [[10, 10], [4, 14]]
      - [[14, 4], [6, 6]]
adjust:
    cell_0_0:
      scale: 1.4
    cell_0_1:
      scale: 1.4
    cell_1_0:
      scale: 1.4
    cell_1_1:
      scale: 1.4
    row_label_0:
      scale: 1.3
    row_label_1:
      scale: 1.3
    col_label_0:
      scale: 1.3
    col_label_1:
      scale: 1.3
    row_player_label:
      scale: 1.3
      move: [-4, 6]
    col_player_label:
      scale: 1.3
sources:
    - id: sep_pd
      title: Prisoner's Dilemma
      authors: Kuhn, S. (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)
      year: 2019
      finding: Puzzles with this structure were devised and discussed by Merrill Flood and Melvin Dresher at RAND in 1950; Albert Tucker gave the name and the prison-sentence story.
      url: https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/prisoner-dilemma/

    text t_1_1 "6, 6" #3d3833 x 615 y 285 font_size 44 hidden true
    text t_1_0 "14, 4" #3d3833 x 385 y 285 font_size 44 hidden true
    text t_0_1 "4, 14" #3d3833 x 615 y 435 font_size 44 hidden true
    text t_0_0 "10, 10" #3d3833 x 385 y 435 font_size 44 hidden true
    text unit "profit, $bn a year (say)" #8f887c x 500 y 170 font_size 22 hidden true
    text nash_cue "Nash equilibrium" #b5482e x 860 y 300 font_size 28 hidden true
    mark miss around cell_0_0 kind circle hidden true
    portrait tucker cites ["sep_pd"] x 870 y 420 width 190 of "Albert W. Tucker" cameo true hidden true

    card title "The table"

B: Shrink the cartel to two members, A and B. Each chooses: keep to its quota, or pump more.
    draw grid row_player_label col_player_label row_label_0 row_label_1 col_label_0 col_label_1

B: Profits, (@draw unit t_0_0@) in billions a year, illustrative. If both keep the quota, ten each.

B: If A pumps more while B (@draw t_1_0@) holds back, A sells extra oil at a price that is still hi (@draw t_0_1@)gh: fourteen. B is left with four.

B: An (@draw t_1_1@)d if both pump, the price sinks: six each.

So say I'm A, and B keeps its promise. What should I do?
    focus t_0_0 t_1_0 col_label_0

    ask "B keeps its quota. Tap the cell that is better for A." choose ["t_0_0","t_1_0"] answer t_1_0
        right: Pump more: for A, fourteen beats ten.
        wrong: Read A's number, the first in each cell. It's pump more: fourteen beats ten.

B: And if B pumps more too? Then pumping gets you six instead of four.
    focus t_0_1 t_1_1 col_label_1

So whatever B does, I'm better off pumping.
    highlight row_label_1 effect circle

B: That's a dominant strategy: the best move whatever the other  (@highlight t_0_1 t_1_1 effect box@)side does. And B's table is (@hide t_0_0 t_0_1 t_1_0 t_1_1@) th (@draw cell_0_0 cell_0_1 cell_1_0 cell_1_1 parallel true@)e mirror image. The underlines mark each side's best replies.

Then we both pump, and (@draw nash_1_1@) land here: six each.

B: A Nash e (@draw nash_cue@)quilibrium: neither of you can do better by changing only your own move.

    erase nash_cue

Yet ten each was sitting righ (@draw miss@)t there. Both would be better off, and neither will go there alone.

B: Merrill Flood and Melvin Dresher found this shape at RAND in 1950. Albert Tucker  (@draw tucker@)named it the prisoner's dilemma, after a story about two suspects.

    erase tucker

## Change the payoffs
use: payoff_matrix
with:
    row_player: Member A
    col_player: Member B
    row_strategies:
      - Keep quota
      - Pump more
    col_strategies:
      - Keep quota
      - Pump more
    payoffs:
      - [[10, 10], [4, 14]]
      - [[14, 4], [6, 6]]
adjust:
    cell_0_0:
      scale: 1.4
    cell_0_1:
      scale: 1.4
    cell_1_0:
      scale: 1.4
    cell_1_1:
      scale: 1.4
    row_label_0:
      scale: 1.3
    row_label_1:
      scale: 1.3
    col_label_0:
      scale: 1.3
    col_label_1:
      scale: 1.3
    row_player_label:
      scale: 1.3
      move: [-4, 6]
    col_player_label:
      scale: 1.3
sources:
    - id: mengel2018
      title: 'Risk and Temptation: A Meta-study on Prisoner''s Dilemma Games'
      authors: Mengel, F.
      year: 2018
      finding: Across 96 prisoner's dilemma studies with more than 3,500 participants, the average cooperation rate in one-shot and stranger-matching treatments ranges from 4% to 84% with a mean of 37%; the risk of cooperating unilaterally best explains the variation.
      doi: 10.1111/ecoj.12548

    text fine "fine: −5 for pumping" #b5482e x 500 y 160 font_size 36 hidden true
    text law "cartels: illegal" #b5482e x 500 y 160 font_size 36 hidden true
    scale coop cites ["mengel2018"] x 110 y 85 width 620 label "cooperate, playing once" unit % min 0 max 100 value 37 hidden true
    text range "range 4–84%" #8f887c cites ["mengel2018"] x 865 y 115 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Change the payoffs"

Pumping wins only because of the numbers in the table. Change one, and the story may change.
    draw grid row_player_label col_player_label row_label_0 row_label_1 col_label_0 col_label_1 cell_0_0 cell_0_1 cell_1_0 cell_1_1 nash_1_1 parallel true

B: Say the cartel could fi (@draw fine@)ne any member five billion for pumping over its quota.

Then keeping the quota is A's best move whatever B does. B's too. The equilibrium jumps to ten each.
    animate payoffs.1.0.0 9 payoffs.1.1.0 1 payoffs.0.1.1 9 payoffs.1.1.1 1 duration 2.5 ghost true

B: So the trap isn't in the players. It's in the payoffs. Fix those, and rational players cooperate.
    highlight cell_0_0 effect circle

Try it: change what pumping pays, and find where the equilibrium moves.
    explore params ["payoffs.1.0.0","payoffs.1.1.0","payoffs.0.1.1","payoffs.1.1.1"]

    erase fine

    animate payoffs.1.0.0 14 payoffs.1.1.0 6 payoffs.0.1.1 14 payoffs.1.1.1 6 duration 1.5

B: But no court will enforce a cartel' (@draw law@)s fine. In most countries the deal itself is illegal.

On purpose. For buyers, the rivals' dilemma is good news: it's what competition looks like.
    highlight nash_1_1

    erase law

B: Now my real objection. Put real people in this game, strangers, playing once, for real money. Do they all pump?
    focus cell_0_0 cell_1_1

Guess first: what share cooperate?
    draw coop

    ask "What share of strangers cooperate, playing once? Click the line." on coop store g
        right: Close: you said {g}; on average it is {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; on average it is {g.true}.

About thirty-seven percent, on average, across ninety-six lab studies pooled in one meta-study. Theory says zero.
    highlight coop

B: And it ranges from four pe (@draw range@)rcent to eighty-four, depending on the payoffs: fewer cooperate when being the lone cooperator costs more.

    erase coop range

## Trapped by the payoffs
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: OPEC could have used you in 1985.
    good: You'd spot a dilemma before signing a cartel deal.
    poor: 'Ask the one question: does breaking the deal pay whatever the other side does?'
    none: Even game theorists skip a question now and then.
use: payoff_matrix
with:
    row_player: Member A
    col_player: Member B
    row_strategies:
      - Keep quota
      - Pump more
    col_strategies:
      - Keep quota
      - Pump more
    payoffs:
      - [[10, 10], [4, 14]]
      - [[14, 4], [6, 6]]
adjust:
    cell_0_0:
      scale: 1.4
    cell_0_1:
      scale: 1.4
    cell_1_0:
      scale: 1.4
    cell_1_1:
      scale: 1.4
    row_label_0:
      scale: 1.3
    row_label_1:
      scale: 1.3
    col_label_0:
      scale: 1.3
    col_label_1:
      scale: 1.3
    row_player_label:
      scale: 1.3
      move: [-4, 6]
    col_player_label:
      scale: 1.3
sources:
    - id: mengel2018
      title: 'Risk and Temptation: A Meta-study on Prisoner''s Dilemma Games'
      authors: Mengel, F.
      year: 2018
      finding: Across 96 prisoner's dilemma studies, the mean cooperation rate in one-shot and stranger treatments is 37%.
      doi: 10.1111/ecoj.12548

    text sure1 "Logic: consensus" #3d3833 x 500 y 182 font_size 36 hidden true
    text sure2 "Real rivals: contested" #b5482e cites ["mengel2018"] x 500 y 140 font_size 36 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch math work [{"tex":"T=14,\\ R=10,\\ P=6,\\ S=4"},{"tex":"T>R,\\ P>S:\\ \\text{pump wins}"},{"tex":"R>P:\\ \\text{both lose}"}] x 500 y 380 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Trapped by the payoffs"

So how sure are we? The logic is airtight: with these payoffs, pumping is dominant for both.
    draw grid row_player_label col_player_label row_label_0 row_label_1 col_label_0 col_label_1 cell_0_0 cell_0_1 cell_1_0 cell_1_1 nash_1_1 parallel true

That part is consensus.
    draw sure1

B: How often real rivals are stuck in it is another matter. Many find way (@draw sure2@)s out, and a third of lab players cooperate anyway. That's contested.

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want to see the general rule behind the table?

@math

    erase mathq

    fade grid row_player_label col_player_label row_label_0 row_label_1 col_label_0 col_label_1 cell_0_0 cell_0_1 cell_1_0 cell_1_1 nash_1_1 sure1 sure2 to 0.15

Call the payoffs temptation, reward, punishment and sucker: fourteen, ten, six and four.
    draw math_box math_line_1

If temptation beats reward, and punishment beats sucker, defecting is dominant.
    draw math_line_2

And if reward beats punishment, both defecting leaves both worse off. That's the whole dilemma.
    draw math_line_3

    erase math

@test

    erase mathq

    fade grid row_player_label col_player_label row_label_0 row_label_1 col_label_0 col_label_1 cell_0_0 cell_0_1 cell_1_0 cell_1_1 nash_1_1 sure1 sure2 to 1

Three new cases to try it on.
    erase sure1 sure2

    quiz "Two Harbourtown bakeries each earn most if both price high, but each wins customers by undercutting, and no deal can be enforced. What do you expect?"
        right: Both undercut: whatever the rival does, undercutting pays more, so both end up with low prices and lower profits.
        wrong: Ask what Mia does best if her rival prices high, and then if it prices low. Both undercut.
        * Both keep prices high
        + Both undercut
        * They take turns

    hide grid row_player_label col_player_label row_label_0 row_label_1 col_label_0 col_label_1 cell_0_0 cell_0_1 cell_1_0 cell_1_1 nash_1_1

    cards pd select "Prisoner's dilemma" check each
        * "Two firms' ad budgets" in true
        * "An arms race" in true
        * "Friends picking a café" in false
        * "Swapping apples for pears" in false

    ask "Which of these are prisoner's dilemmas? Tap every one." on pd store f stage own voice b
        right: All right: firms' ads and an arms race. In each, every side gains by escalating whatever the other does, and both lose if both do.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try. In a dilemma each side gains by defecting whatever the other does, yet both lose if both do. The dilemmas: the ads and the arms race.

    erase pd

    show grid row_player_label col_player_label row_label_0 row_label_1 col_label_0 col_label_1 cell_0_0 cell_0_1 cell_1_0 cell_1_1 nash_1_1

    quiz "Harbourtown's fishers agree a catch limit. What would most help them keep it?"
        right: The fine: it changes the payoffs, so keeping the limit becomes each fisher's best move.
        wrong: A promise changes no number in the table. The council fine does.
        * A promise over a beer
        + A council fine larger than the gain from cheating
        * Telling each that the others will cheat

So why do rivals fail to cooperate? Not from stupidity. When breaking the deal pays whatever the other side does, rational rivals break it, and all lose.
    highlight nash_1_1

B: Unless something changes the payoffs. And one thing might: what if they meet again tomorrow?
    highlight cell_0_0 effect circle

    pause 0.3

